motanka-core
============

Source of the motanka-core package: the motanka command, upstream Omarchy
vendored unmodified at the stable Upstream_Pin, the compatibility layer that
runs it on Ubuntu, and the Motanka login session. Installed as
/usr/share/doc/motanka-core/README.

Building
--------

test/vm/build builds it inside a build VM. On the host it first runs
tools/fetch-upstream, which downloads the pinned Upstream tarball into
~/.cache/motanka-vm/upstream-tarballs/ and checks its SHA-256. The tarball
becomes the dpkg-source component omarchy/ (motanka-core_<version>.orig-
omarchy.tar.gz); packaging/build.sh unpacks it with tools/vendor, which
checks it against upstream.pin again. debian/control is generated per suite
from debian/control.in, and the Depends listed in debian/pkgmap-depends are
resolved from packages.yaml. debian/core-install.sh does the build steps.

Builds are Dev_Builds (they ship /opt/motanka/share/motanka/DEV_BUILD)
unless the build profile pkg.motanka.release is set.

The session
-----------

/usr/share/wayland-sessions/motanka.desktop runs
/opt/motanka/libexec/motanka/session. The launcher refuses to start while
another graphical session of the same user is active, or when the GPU
driver is not supported; the reason is in the journal
(journalctl -t motanka). It sets XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP=Motanka:Hyprland,
XDG_SESSION_DESKTOP=motanka, DCONF_PROFILE=motanka,
OMARCHY_PATH=/usr/share/omarchy and puts /opt/motanka/bin first on PATH and
/opt/motanka/share first in XDG_DATA_DIRS, and then starts the Stack's
Hyprland through the Stack's uwsm and start-hyprland, as upstream Omarchy
does.

Once the session runs, the launcher starts motanka-session.target, which is
bound to graphical-session.target. Its unit motanka-session-cleanup.service
gives the systemd user manager back its earlier environment when the
session ends. Desktop settings of the session are in the dconf database
"motanka" (/etc/dconf/profile/motanka; defaults in
/etc/dconf/db/motanka.d/), which no other session reads.

/usr/share/omarchy, where upstream Omarchy expects its files, links to
/opt/motanka/share/omarchy-view: its bin is /opt/motanka/bin (the command
wrappers) and its other entries link into the unmodified vendored tree.

Per-user setup
--------------

Before the compositor starts, the launcher runs
/opt/motanka/libexec/motanka/user-setup. At the first login it copies
upstream's config/ tree into ~/.config according to
/opt/motanka/share/motanka/home-paths.tsv, creates the dconf database
~/.config/dconf/motanka and runs the upstream per-user scripts that
/opt/motanka/share/motanka/user-scripts.tsv allows. Files that other
sessions read, such as ~/.bashrc, ~/.gitconfig and the GTK settings, are
left unchanged, and existing application configs are kept. Every write is
recorded first in ~/.local/state/motanka/ledger, replaced files are backed
up there, and an interrupted setup resumes at the next login. Later logins
find the setup complete and skip it.

Shell integration
-----------------

At the first login motanka-shell-integration.service shows one
notification asking whether to load the Omarchy-compatible bash setup in
terminals of the Motanka session. Only "Enable" changes a file: it adds
one block between "# >>> motanka >>>" and "# <<< motanka <<<" to
~/.bashrc. The question is not asked again; run
/opt/motanka/libexec/motanka/shell-integration accept (or decline) to
answer later.

Updates and rollback
--------------------

"sudo motanka update" takes a snapshot in /var/lib/motanka/snapshots/
(the installed Motanka packages with their .debs, and an archive of each
user's ledgered files and Motanka folders; the newest 3 are kept), upgrades
the Motanka packages without changing packages Motanka does not own
(--system allows upgrades of them), and runs the invoking user's pending
upstream migrations. /opt/motanka/share/motanka/migrations.tsv, generated
by tools/classify-migrations, says for each migration whether it runs
unchanged (ok), runs an Ubuntu version (override) or is recorded as done
without running (n/a). A failing migration stops the update and keeps the
snapshot. "sudo motanka rollback" reinstalls the snapshot's packages from
its stored .debs and restores your files from it. The Omarchy menu's update
item runs "sudo motanka update".
